  "practice_data": {
   "method_card": {
    "steps": [
     "Magnification = image size ÷ actual size (no units — it is a ratio)",
     "To find actual size: actual = image size ÷ magnification",
     "To find image size: image = actual size × magnification",
     "Unit conversions: 1 mm = 1,000 µm; 1 µm = 1,000 nm. Multiply going to smaller units; divide going to larger."
    ],
    "title": "Magnification and Unit Conversions",
    "content": "<p>The magnification formula has three forms — choose based on what you need to find.</p><p><strong>Key rules:</strong></p><ul><li>Always ensure image size and actual size are in the <em>same units</em> before dividing</li><li>Magnification has NO units — it is a pure ratio</li><li>To go from mm → µm: multiply by 1,000. To go from µm → mm: divide by 1,000</li></ul><p><strong>Size reference points:</strong></p><ul><li>Plant cell: 10–100 µm</li><li>Animal cell: 10–30 µm</li><li>Mitochondrion: 1–10 µm</li><li>Bacterium: 1–10 µm</li><li>Virus: 20–300 nm</li></ul><p><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong></p><ul><li>Using different units for image and actual size (e.g. one in mm and the other in µm)</li><li>Forgetting to take the final unit conversion step</li><li>Multiplying by magnification instead of dividing when finding actual size</li></ul>"
   },
